# Coreset CPU pilot: metric and strong-comparator audit

Prospective development protocol, fixed before this project's numerical results.
Question: Is the native l1 coreset score reproduced by an independent sorted-prefix cost calculation, and is there a useful cost opportunity beyond an optimized exact coordinate median?
This is a calibration/milestone, not a new algorithm or independent confirmation.

Sources: Stable Coresets ICLR2026 Section 6 and Eq.6; author commit 48f0233eb65269b8552eb163f275627e5447921d, l1_coresets.py and experiment1_comparison.ipynb cells 2/4.
Data: official NYC yellow_tripdata_2024-01.parquet; float64 columns, drop RatecodeID, drop missing rows in those columns, preserve row order, no rescaling. First 10,000 rows reproduce the documented narrow native scope. First 100,000 rows provide a separate scaling diagnostic; neither is independent confirmation.
Arms: exact coordinate median, uniform-with-replacement coreset size 150/300/1000, author's one-step and two-step importance samplers at 10,000 rows. Importance probabilities are prepared once per input, using original code. Report their setup separately and include it in unamortized totals. At 100,000 rows compare exact and uniform only; no broad superiority claim against omitted importance arms.
Seeds: three prespecified development seeds 11,29,47 for sample variability. These are not claimed sufficient for significance. Preparation seed 7. The full source file and preparation routine stay unchanged.
Metric: (full-data cost of coreset solution - optimal coordinate-median cost)/optimal cost, original l1 metric. Validate original scorer against sorted per-coordinate prefixes and search positions, relative tolerance 1e-10, absolute cost tolerance 1e-7; inspect disagreements, do not silently change tolerance.
Timing: loading, preparation, sampling+solving, exact solving, scoring separately, include all costs and peak RSS. Shared-host timings are descriptive, not controlled speedup evidence. Exact lower/upper median representation may differ while preserving objective value. Numerical threads1; science process1; 4GiB RLIMIT_AS; project limit8,000,000,000 bytes; timeout180s.
Decision: invalid metric -> repair; exact baseline already cheap -> do not pursue speed from weak comparison; useful cost gap -> test one justified hypothesis with real data and fair total cost. Uniform stochastic outcomes may be nonmonotonic. All rows retained. Preserve previous user-reported pilot separately.
Residual work: closest prior mechanisms, selected hypothesis, uncertainty-based design, nontrivial regimes/other author tasks, honest end-to-end cost, and fresh confirmation. Full paper reproduction and downstream LLM applications are outside this pilot.
